  "instruction": "From the Australian Census data, for all persons and all private dwellings, which state out of NSW, Victoria, and Queensland had the smallest decrease in proportion of median weekly rent to median weekly household income from 2016 to 2021?",
  "answer": "Based on the ABS Census QuickStats data for 2016 and 2021, here are the calculations:\n\n**2016 Census (median weekly rent / median weekly household income):**\n- NSW: $380 / $1,486 = **25.57%**\n- Victoria: $325 / $1,419 = **22.90%**\n- Queensland: $330 / $1,402 = **23.54%**\n\n**2021 Census (median weekly rent / median weekly household income):**\n- NSW: $420 / $1,829 = **22.96%**\n- Victoria: $370 / $1,759 = **21.03%**\n- Queensland: $365 / $1,675 = **21.79%**\n\n**Decrease in proportion (2016 → 2021):**\n- NSW: 25.57% → 22.96% = **2.61 percentage points decrease**\n- Victoria: 22.90% → 21.03% = **1.87 percentage points decrease**\n- Queensland: 23.54% → 21.79% = **1.75 percentage points decrease**\n\n**Answer: Queensland** had the smallest decrease in the proportion of median weekly rent to median weekly household income from 2016 to 2021 (1.75 percentage points).",
